          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01UjB1Y7vzAJLpZ7Ni9YK4nY",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/autoscaling.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Autoscaling\\ndescription: Automatically adjusting the number of running replicas of a service based on observed load, with stabilization to prevent flapping.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Autoscaling\\n  - tech/Kubernetes\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Two sources: initial definition and HPA stabilization behavior; no contradictions.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\nAutoscaling adjusts replica count for a service automatically instead of requiring a\\nhuman to resize it manually. The controller observes some signal \\u2014 CPU/memory\\nutilization, queue depth, or a custom metric \\u2014 and computes a desired replica count on\\nan interval.\\n\\n## Kubernetes Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA)\\n\\nHPA is Kubernetes\\u0027s built-in pod-scaling controller. It polls the metrics API at regular\\nintervals, compares observed CPU/memory utilization (or custom metrics) against a target\\nvalue, and computes a desired replica count. To avoid rapid oscillation between scale-up\\nand scale-down decisions (flapping), HPA applies a **stabilization window** \\u2014 a time\\nperiod during which the controller will not issue conflicting scaling actions. This\\nprevents unnecessary churn and resource thrashing.\\n\\nHPA scales *replica count* only \\u2014 it does not provision new nodes in the cluster. When\\npod-level scaling alone cannot absorb load (all nodes are full), the separate **Cluster\\nAutoscaler** controller provisions new nodes to meet resource requests. HPA and Cluster\\nAutoscaler work together but remain independent controllers with distinct responsibilities.\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
